L(H_all) - L(H_sch) in bits (positive: the posterior prefers the instance schema), rule codes:
  fixed   : uniform over 10 rules, as in c4_bdtrc.py (log2 10 per extra step)
  shared  : learned rule distribution, one context for all derivation nodes
  depth   : learned rule distribution, context = depth of the node in the derivation tree
       n      fixed     shared      depth  depth/log2(n)
      10       33.2       23.4       12.5           3.75
      30       99.7       63.8       18.8           3.83
     100      332.2      203.9       26.3           3.96
     300      996.6      604.0       33.3           4.05
    1000     3321.9     2004.0       41.1           4.12
    3000     9965.8     6004.0       48.2           4.17
   10000    33219.3    20004.0       56.0           4.22
  100000   332192.8   200004.0       71.0           4.27
 1000000  3321928.1  2000004.0       85.9           4.31
Reading: with a fixed or a shared rule code the penalty is linear (3.3 or about 2 bits per datum); with a
depth-conditioned rule code it is (R-1)/2 * log2 n + O(1) = 4.5 log2 n: polynomial posterior odds, not
exponential.  The direction (H_sch favoured) survives; the exponential rate is a property of the rule code.
